Elixir calls every Erlang/OTP module with an ATOM module -- `:ets.insert`,
`:httpc.request`, `:gen_tcp.send` -- and `_handle_dot_call` required the dot
node to carry an `alias` child. An atom parses as `atom`, so the handler
returned before emitting anything: not an untyped edge and not a misbound one
but NO EDGE AT ALL, an INV-linub L2 failure that no downstream receiver
evidence can rescue. The parent catalogue's OTP rows were present and
reachable by nothing; `io_primitives/elixir.yaml`'s header has claimed since
2026-04 that they are "covered for the atom-access path", which was true of
the rows and false of the path. That header is corrected with a date.

SCOPE BY PARITY WITH THE WORKING ALIAS PATH, NOT BY THE CONSTRUCT. Probing the
grammar rather than trusting the filing found SIX early-returning dot shapes,
not the one filed. Fixed: the shape whose module is written literally at the
call site. Refused and pinned as known gaps: a variable receiver
(`mod.fun()`), a module attribute (`@attr.thing()`), a quoted atom
(`:"Elixir.Foo".bar()`, zero corpus sites and an undecided
`:"Elixir.Foo"`/`Foo` normalisation) and `__MODULE__.helper()`. Refused
CORRECTLY, also pinned: a dot call with no named callee -- `f.()`,
`(fn x -> x end).()`, and the operator forms `Kernel.+(1,2)` / `:erlang.+(1,2)`,
which carry a receiver this analyzer now handles but put an
`operator_identifier` where the function name goes.

THE COLON IS STRIPPED, WHICH IS THE LOAD-BEARING HALF. `_module_matches` is
component-aware and does no colon stripping, so a module slot of `:ets`
matches the catalogue's `ets` row not at all -- the edge would exist and reach
nothing, which is exactly the failure INV-linub exists to catch. Stripping is
also the cross-language parity answer: the erlang analyzer, calling these same
OTP modules, emits `erlang:ets:0-0:insert:function`.

ATOM CALLS BYPASS FIRST-PARTY RESOLUTION BY CONSTRUCTION. Those branches end
in an `any(module_dot in k ...)` SUBSTRING test -- the defect the sibling item
WI-kafor files -- and `"ets."` is a substring of any project key such as
`Foo.Sockets.insert`, so routing atoms through them would bind `:ets.insert`
to a first-party function. A test pins that a project defining `Foo.Sockets`
does not capture it. A second test pins the landmine in the other direction:
`mod.fun(1)` puts TWO `identifier` children on the dot node, so
`find_child_by_type(dot_node, "identifier")` returns `mod` -- the RECEIVER --
and loosening the guard generally would emit an edge named after a local
variable.

